ate shift in the national midterm environment or further Republican outside spending could pull the race back to a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Gary Peters (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Michigan's open Senate seat features Democrat Abdul El-Sayed against Republican Mike Rogers after Gary Peters's retirement. The state sits at partisan parity, making this one of the cycle's most contested races. Polls since late August consistently show El-Sayed leading, with margins ranging from two to seven points and the newest surveys at the higher end. Forecasters are split between Tossup and Lean D, while prediction markets give El-Sayed roughly a 70-73% chance of winning.\n\nMoney is a complicating factor: El-Sayed has outraised Rogers, but Republican-aligned outside groups have spent heavily against him, and Rogers holds a cash-on-hand edge. Harris's campaigning and favorable recent coverage have boosted El-Sayed, though the race remains within normal polling error. A modest Democratic edge with real uncertainty best fits the evidence.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7769, "margin": 3.59}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7646, "margin": 3.5}]}